> Here is an abstract for a grant proposal:
> ===
> Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) are among the most persistent and pervasive environmental health threats of the 21st century. Nearly half of U.S. tap water contains detectable PFAS, and EPA’s UCMR 5 program is generating the first nationally representative monitoring data for 29 compounds in public water systems. Epidemiological studies consistently link prenatal PFAS exposure to adverse birth outcomes, yet economics lacks a rigorous causal identification framework that connects industrial pollution sources to downstream health damages.
> 
> This project develops the first integrated causal architecture for PFAS, combining methods from economics, hydrology, and environmental science. Our research design proceeds in three stages: (1) Source → Environment: we estimate the causal effect of facility discharges and spill events on local PFAS concentrations using event-study and difference-in-differences frameworks; (2) Environment → Exposure: we exploit the structure of hydrologic networks to trace concentrations across multiple water systems, using rainfall and river-flow shocks as natural instruments; (3) Exposure → Health: leveraging national natality data, we implement two-stage instrumental variable regressions to quantify the effect of PFAS exposure on infant health outcomes.
> 
> 
> 
> 
> The contribution is twofold: establishing a causal framework linking emissions, concentrations, exposure, and health, and providing the first national evidence of PFAS impacts on birth outcomes. By advancing environmental health economics, the project closes a critical gap and offers a replicable framework for studying persistent pollutants.
> ===
